  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Dewavrin entered specialist retail by founding King-Musique in 1972. The chain aimed to make hi-fi equipment accessible to younger customers and expanded rapidly to dozens of outlets. Weak inventory control and reliance on supplier credit eventually forced him to withdraw. The experience gave him a detailed view of both the appeal and operating risks of high-volume electronics retail.\n\nHe applied those lessons to Surcouf, created with Hervé Collin in 1992. Its Paris store presented computers and components in a deliberately open, fair-like environment where customers could compare brands, watch demonstrations, assemble machines and obtain repairs. The concept grew into Europe’s largest specialist computer store, attracting millions of visitors. Dewavrin and Collin later sold their controlling interest to Pinault-Printemps-Redoute, converting an unconventional retail format into a substantial exit.",
    "html": "<p>Dewavrin entered specialist retail by founding King-Musique in 1972. The chain aimed to make hi-fi equipment accessible to younger customers and expanded rapidly to dozens of outlets. Weak inventory control and reliance on supplier credit eventually forced him to withdraw. The experience gave him a detailed view of both the appeal and operating risks of high-volume electronics retail.</p>\r\n<p>He applied those lessons to Surcouf, created with Hervé Collin in 1992. Its Paris store presented computers and components in a deliberately open, fair-like environment where customers could compare brands, watch demonstrations, assemble machines and obtain repairs. The concept grew into Europe’s largest specialist computer store, attracting millions of visitors. Dewavrin and Collin later sold their controlling interest to Pinault-Printemps-Redoute, converting an unconventional retail format into a substantial exit.</p>\r\n",
